{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":14,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1864"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1864"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_362955411#11_336506760","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1864","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_362955411#11_336506760\" , \"end_char\": 8640.000000 , \"headings\": \"Legionary Legionary Contents History Inception: <cover>Marius's reforms During the Principate Decline Functions Immunes Recruitment Equipment Battle and combat Maintaining morale Prelude to battle Fighting style Pay and conditions Training and discipline Diet Healthcare Other legionaries See also References General books Other books External links \" , \"segment\": \"This meant that levees remained a significant part of the Roman legions. With the state providing the equipment to the recruits and no property requirements, even the poorest Roman citizens were able to join the legions. However, the army was viewed as an honorable and valued profession. With a steady pay, good retirement benefits, and even certain legal advantages, a legionary had many perks that common citizens found desirable. As such, though poor citizens could join the military, </cover>members from across the plebeian class were found in the Roman legions. Indeed, the army served as one of the few avenues of upward mobility in the Roman world. The army actively sought out recruits with useful skills such ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1567251276#18_3198754316","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":13},"model_calls":14,"document_judgments":11,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
