{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":16,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1865"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1865"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_1432636283#3_2192084847","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1865","rank":3,"snippet":": \"After the Punic wars, the Roman state got way too big for the antiquated Republican institutions to govern. The senate governed provinces through appointed proconsuls, who held dictatorial power within their designated provinces, and this method of government proved to be ineffective. Professionalization of the army. During the early and mid republic, roman soldiers were citizen farmers, who fought for the state as their civic duty. In addition, all the conquered areas had to provide a certain number of troops for the <cover>roman army as a part of their alliance with the roman state. These soldiers were loyal to the state. With Marian reforms, the job of a soldier became paid and landless </cover>poor were given the opportunity to enlist. The weird quirk of this system was that the state didn’t actually pay the soldiers, the soldiers were paid by their general, and much of the pay was war booty, so the soldiers were loyal to their general rather than the state. These generals then became more powerful than the senate, because they had armies and the senate didn’t. Civil wars between Rome’s generals were bound to lead to an eventual winner, who would take ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3718936904#1_3819992011","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":11},"model_calls":16,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
