{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":24,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1869"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1869"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_378001354#7_784192484","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1869","rank":4,"snippet":"Roman Republic (Bartram, 2018). Marius’s motivations behind the implementation of the reforms were forced upon as Rome had suffered four straight significant defeats to the Celtic and Germanic tribes. Marius faced a major issue as he needed more soldiers to combat the invading tribes. For Marius to have enough soldiers, he eliminated the land requirement that was present that only allowed the wealthier classes to represent the Roman army (Nofi, 2019). This opportunity opened the military to any Roman male citizen, including the Plebeian classes. <cover>Marius improved Roman military training by using various techniques developed by ancient gladiators. These military reforms also replaced the original three soldier classes to many cohorts of men who were all armed with a gladius (short sword) and used the eagle to formalise the standard of all legions </cover>in the army. Marius’s motivations behind the reforms did exactly what was wished, to professionalise and revolutionise Rome’s military (Matthew, 2006).\" , \"start_char\": 4624.000000 , \"title\": \"Impact of Gaius Marius’ Reforms\" , \"url\": \"https://www.ukessays.com/essays/history/impact-of-gaius-marius-reforms.php\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_378001354#8_784194451","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_241157139#7_545155934","rank":2,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_241157139#8_545157995","rank":3,"relevance":2}]},"time":6},"model_calls":24,"document_judgments":19,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
