{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":25,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1869"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1869"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_297879003#1_618347992","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1869","rank":5,"snippet":"BC-284 AD) Later Centurions Related pages References Other websites References \" , \"segment\": \"It was usually accompanied by attached units of auxiliaries, who were not Roman citizens. They provided cavalry, ranged troops and skirmishers to complement the legion's heavy infantry. The size of a typical legion varied during the history of ancient Rome. It had a complement of 4,200 legionaries in the republican period of Rome. In the imperial period, the full complement was 5,500 men split into 10 <cover>cohorts of 480 men each. The first cohort was at double strength with 800 men. The remaining 220 were 120 cavalry plus technical staff. Rome did not have a standing army until the reforms of Gaius Marius about 107 BC. Legions instead were created, used, and disbanded again. In the time of the early Roman </cover>Empire, there were usually about 25–35 standing legions plus their auxiliaries, with more raised as needed.\" , \"start_char\": 575.000000 , \"title\": \"Roman legion - Simple English Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\" , \"url\": \"https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_legion\" }"},"time":14},"model_calls":25,"document_judgments":20,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
