{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":37,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"marian reforms\" professional army)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"marian reforms\" professional army)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_1477560347#6_2441143657","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1880","rank":1,"snippet":"Military Science that covers the choosing of suitable recruits, weapons training, training in battle manoeuvres, and other practical issues that relate to the Roman Army. Centurion Luc Viatour / www.Lucnix.be (CC BY-NC-SA) Recruitment The citizen soldiers of the manipular army would be enrolled for a specific amount of time, rather than signing up for years of service as they would do in the Imperial period. This meant that the legions of the Roman Republic had no long continual existences because they were disbanded after the campaign they had been serving on was finished. The result of the <cover>Marian reforms was a professional standing army </cover>for the Roman State, or in the coming years, individual generals who gained the loyalty of their legions. Remove Ads Advertisement The majority of Roman soldiers would have been recruited around the age of 18-20 years, and in the 1st century CE, there is a decrease in Italian recruits as recruits from the provinces increased.\" , \"start_char\": 4464.000000 , \"title\": \"Roman Army - World History Encyclopedia\" , \"url\": \"http://www.ancient.eu/Roman_Army/\" }"},"time":97},"model_calls":37,"document_judgments":28,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
