{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1886"},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1886"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1886"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_378001354#7_784192484","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1886","rank":3,"snippet":"Roman Republic (Bartram, 2018). Marius’s motivations behind the implementation of the reforms were forced upon as Rome had suffered four straight significant defeats to the Celtic and Germanic tribes. Marius faced a major issue as he needed more soldiers to combat the invading tribes. For Marius to have enough soldiers, he eliminated the land requirement that was present that only allowed the wealthier classes to represent the Roman army (Nofi, 2019). This opportunity opened the military to any Roman male citizen, including the Plebeian classes. Marius improved Roman military training by using various techniques developed by ancient gladiators. These military reforms also replaced the original three soldier classes to many <cover>cohorts of men who were all armed with a gladius (short sword) and used the eagle to formalise the standard of all legions in the army. Marius’s motivations behind the reforms did exactly what was wished, to professionalise and revolutionise Rome’s military (Matthew, 2006).\" , \"start_char\": 4624.000000 , \"title\": \"Impact of Gaius Marius’ </cover>Reforms\" , \"url\": \"https://www.ukessays.com/essays/history/impact-of-gaius-marius-reforms.php\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_378001354#7_784192484","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1886"},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1886"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1886"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_297879003#1_618347992","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1886","rank":4,"snippet":"BC-284 AD) Later Centurions Related pages References Other websites References \" , \"segment\": \"It was usually accompanied by attached units of auxiliaries, who were not Roman citizens. They provided cavalry, ranged troops and skirmishers to complement the legion's heavy infantry. The size of a typical legion varied during the history of ancient Rome. It had a complement of 4,200 legionaries in the republican period of Rome. In the imperial period, the full complement was 5,500 men split into 10 <cover>cohorts of 480 men each. The first cohort was at double strength with 800 men. The remaining 220 were 120 cavalry plus technical staff. Rome did not have a standing army until the reforms of Gaius Marius about 107 BC. Legions </cover>instead were created, used, and disbanded again. In the time of the early Roman Empire, there were usually about 25–35 standing legions plus their auxiliaries, with more raised as needed.\" , \"start_char\": 575.000000 , \"title\": \"Roman legion - Simple English Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\" , \"url\": \"https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_legion\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_297879003#1_618347992","rank":4,"relevance":2}]},"time":6},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
