{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-129925","model_call":49,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1888"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1888"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_960272640#3_1707193337","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1888","rank":2,"snippet":"master? relating to or involving money \" , \"segment\": \"Freebase (0.00 / 0 votes) Rate this definition: Sarcina Sarcina is a Latin word meaning a marching pack as carried by Roman Legionaries, the heavy infantry of the Roman legions. Most of a legionary's equipment other than his arms and armour would, in early times, have been consigned to a baggage train and borne by <cover>mules and carts. Image of pack donkey on a Greek wine cup circa 480 BC, Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. However, following the reforms of the Roman General Gaius Marius, the soldiers were expected to carry much of their rations and equipment themselves. This was done to reduce the size of the baggage train and increase the mobility of the army </cover>by allowing the soldiers to move strategically independently of the train. Such was the load of the soldiers that they became known as Marius' Mules. The appearance of the marching pack is known from illustrations on Trajan's Column. Here it can be seen that a legionary's sarcina was carried on a pole called a furca and would have included: ⁕Loculus - a satchel ⁕Cloak bag ⁕Cooking pot ⁕Patera - mess "},"time":14},"model_calls":49,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":12,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
